# Environment variables template for mdmpi_mobile_app
# Copy this file to .env and fill in your actual values
# The .env file is ignored by Git for security

# API Configuration
# Live production host used by /api3/* and all non-/api4 integrations.
API_URL=https://inventory.mdmpi.com.ph

# MDMPI.App production-testing host used only by /api4/* requests.
# API4_URL defaults to API_URL when it is omitted.
API4_URL=https://inventory.mdmpi.com.ph

# Optional local overrides when running MDMPI.App from the full-stack workspace.
# These are used only in debug builds; release builds ignore them.
# API4_URL_WINDOWS=http://localhost:5177
# API4_URL_ANDROID=http://10.0.2.2:5177

API_KEY=your_api_key_here

# WebSocket endpoint api key (optional; falls back to the built-in key).
# The WebSocket path is always /api2/ws; debug builds swap the host for the
# api4 one when the api4 chain resolves to a host other than the live one
# (e.g. API4_URL_WINDOWS above). Release builds always use the live host.
# WS_API_KEY=your_ws_api_key_here

# AI Toolkit configuration (examples)
# Option A: Full Google Generative Language endpoint (not recommended to embed key in URL)
AI_TOOLKIT_GOOGLE_URL=https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta/models/gemini-2.5-flash-lite:generateContent?key=REPLACE_WITH_API_KEY

# Option B: Preferred — provide model and API key separately
AI_TOOLKIT_MODEL=gemini-2.5-flash-lite
AI_TOOLKIT_API_KEY=REPLACE_WITH_API_KEY
AI_TOOLKIT_AUTH_TYPE=bearer
AI_TOOLKIT_PROVIDER=google

# Optional OCR extraction prompt. Handles both scanned documents: the delivery
# receipt (Item Code | Description | Warehouse | Qty | Unit, with batch
# sub-rows) and the Stock Issue Slip (QTY | UM | PART NO. | ITEM DESCRIPTION |
# SERIAL NO. | PTN | REMARKS).
#
# Leave it unset to use InventoryItemRepository.defaultAnalysisPrompt, which is
# identical to the line below; set it only to override. The JSON keys must
# match InventoryItemModel.fromJson and InventoryBatchModel.fromJson.
#
# Must stay on ONE line: dotenv reads line by line, so a real newline here
# silently truncates the prompt. Escape inner quotes as \" and line breaks as
# \n. test/ai_prompt_env_sync_test.dart enforces both.
AI_PROMPT="You are extracting structured data from a delivery document. It is either (A) a delivery receipt whose item table has the columns Item Code | Description | Warehouse | Qty | Unit, or (B) a Stock Issue Slip whose item table has the columns QTY | UM | PART NO. | ITEM DESCRIPTION | SERIAL NO. | PTN | REMARKS. Decide which one it is from the printed column headers, then apply the matching rules below.\nHow to read the table. This procedure is mandatory and overrides any impression the example values give:\n1. The photo may be rotated or skewed. Establish the printed orientation first, so the column headers read left to right, before reading any value.\n2. Read the table ROW BY ROW, never column by column. Fix your attention on one item row, read all of its cells left to right across that single row, emit that row's object, and only then move down to the next row.\n3. It is FORBIDDEN to collect a whole column into a list and then pair the lists up by position. That method silently misaligns every row after the first blank cell and is the single most common way this task is failed. If you have gathered values column-wise, discard them and re-read the document row by row.\n4. Before emitting, count the entries in the QTY column. Your output array must contain exactly that many objects, in printed top-to-bottom order.\n5. For each row, check the horizontal position of every value against the header above it. A value belongs to a key ONLY because it sits under that column. Never infer a key from how a value looks: these documents hold similarly formatted codes in different columns, so format is not evidence of meaning.\n6. A blank cell yields \"\" for that key. It must NEVER pull a value in from a neighbouring column, from the row above, or from the row below. Rows with two or three blanks are normal and must still emit all eight keys.\n7. SERIAL NO. and PTN are two distinct columns whose values look alike. SERIAL NO. is immediately to the right of ITEM DESCRIPTION. PTN is immediately to the right of SERIAL NO. and immediately to the left of REMARKS. A row may have a serial and no PTN. Never place a serial into \"PTN\", and never reuse another row's serial as this row's PTN.\n8. \"Description\" comes only from the ITEM DESCRIPTION column. A PART NO. value, for example one shaped like a word plus a hyphen plus a word, must never appear as a \"Description\". If a row's description cell looks empty, emit \"\" rather than borrowing the part number.\n9. Do NOT extract the REMARKS column. It exists on the printed slip and is useful only as a landmark for locating PTN. Never return a \"Remarks\" key, and never let remarks text spill into \"PTN\" or any other key. A remark is often printed once and centred across several rows, so ignore it wherever it appears.\n10. If a cell is genuinely illegible, emit \"\" for it. Never substitute a value read from anywhere else on the document.\nOutput rules, both types:\n11. Output MUST be a valid JSON array of objects ONLY, with no explanation, no markdown and no code fences.\n12. Each object must contain exactly these keys: \"Item Code\", \"Description\", \"Qty\", \"Unit\", \"Part No.\", \"Serial No.\", \"PTN\", \"batch\".\n13. Use \"\" for any key whose column the document does not have, or whose cell is blank or unreadable. Use [] for \"batch\" when there are no batch details. Never use null.\n14. \"Qty\" and \"Batch Quantity\" must be numbers when parsable, otherwise strings.\n15. Preserve \"Description\" exactly as written.\n16. Ignore all header, footer and company information, and any unrelated or misaligned OCR text.\n17. Ignore filler text that marks the end of the table, such as \"NOTHING FOLLOWS\".\n18. One printed item row is one object. Never merge two item rows, even when they share an item code or a part number. Continuation lines and batch sub-rows are not item rows; the type-specific rules below say how to fold them in.\n19. Return one object per item row, in the printed top-to-bottom order.\n20. If no valid rows are found, return an empty JSON array: [].\nRules for type A, delivery receipt (Item Code | Description | Warehouse | Qty | Unit):\n21. Only process rows under those columns. Do NOT extract or return the Warehouse column.\n22. Item rows may span multiple lines; treat the first line as the main item row containing Item Code, Description, Qty, Unit.\n23. Any immediately following lines containing \"Batch/Serial #\", \"Quantity\", \"Expiry Date\" belong to the most recent item. Map \"Quantity\" to \"Batch Quantity\".\n24. Group all batch entries for one item into its \"batch\" list. Each element inside \"batch\" must be an object with exactly these keys: \"Batch/Serial #\", \"Batch Quantity\", \"Expiry Date\". If several batch entries exist under one item, include all of them.\n25. This document has no part number, serial number or PTN columns, so return \"\" for \"Part No.\", \"Serial No.\" and \"PTN\". Batch serials belong in \"batch\" only; never copy one into \"Serial No.\".\nRules for type B, Stock Issue Slip (QTY | UM | PART NO. | ITEM DESCRIPTION | SERIAL NO. | PTN | REMARKS):\n26. Map the columns as QTY to \"Qty\", UM to \"Unit\", PART NO. to \"Part No.\", ITEM DESCRIPTION to \"Description\", SERIAL NO. to \"Serial No.\" and PTN to \"PTN\". The REMARKS column is ignored entirely.\n27. This document has no item code column, so return \"\" for \"Item Code\".\n28. This document has no batch sub-rows, so always return \"batch\": []. The serial belongs in \"Serial No.\" on the item itself.\n29. A row may wrap its description onto a second line; join the continuation into the same object rather than creating a new one.\n30. PART NO. can be numeric, alphanumeric or hyphenated, and it may be blank on some rows. A blank PART NO. does not mean the row should be skipped or that the description should move left.\nExample output for type A:\n[ { \"Item Code\": \"ODC0004\", \"Description\": \"Control Serum 2, 20 x 5 mL\", \"Qty\": 1.00, \"Unit\": \"BOX\", \"Part No.\": \"\", \"Serial No.\": \"\", \"PTN\": \"\", \"batch\": [ { \"Batch/Serial #\": \"23330939\", \"Batch Quantity\": 1.00, \"Expiry Date\": \"04/21/2026\" }, { \"Batch/Serial #\": \"23330940\", \"Batch Quantity\": 1.00, \"Expiry Date\": \"05/21/2026\" } ] } ]\nExample output for type B. The values are deliberately generic placeholders that show WHERE each column goes, not what real values look like. The second row shows a blank PART NO. and PTN filled with \"\" and no leftward shift:\n[ { \"Item Code\": \"\", \"Description\": \"<text from ITEM DESCRIPTION>\", \"Qty\": 1, \"Unit\": \"UNIT\", \"Part No.\": \"<text from PART NO.>\", \"Serial No.\": \"<text from SERIAL NO.>\", \"PTN\": \"<text from PTN>\", \"batch\": [] }, { \"Item Code\": \"\", \"Description\": \"<text from ITEM DESCRIPTION>\", \"Qty\": 1, \"Unit\": \"PIECE\", \"Part No.\": \"\", \"Serial No.\": \"<text from SERIAL NO.>\", \"PTN\": \"\", \"batch\": [] } ]"

# Toggle to force Google Generative Language flow
USE_GOOGLE_GENERATIVE=false
